    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green. There's only one test class in the repo (`AcceptanceTest`), and it passes. The build also regenerated `OwnerDto` from `openapi.yml` and recompiled the MapStruct mapper, so all my changes compile cleanly.\n\n## Summary\n\n`riskFlag` is a derived, read-only response field, so I implemented it at the response-mapping edge (like the other computed owner fields \u2014 `salutation`, `ownerSegment`, etc.) rather than persisting it. Three files changed:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` `boolean`/`readOnly` property to the `Owner` schema (regenerates `OwnerDto`), documenting the rule.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"riskFlag\", ...)` plus a `default boolean riskFlag(Owner owner)` helper that ORs the three signals:\n   - **possible duplicate** \u2192 `owner.getPossibleDuplicate()` (set by `FlagPossibleDuplicate`)\n   - **city over soft capacity** \u2192 `owner.getCapacityWarning()` (set by `FlagCapacityWarning`, the 40\u201349 soft band below the hard cap of 50)\n   - **email domain disposable-adjacent** \u2192 `OwnerEmail.isDisposableAdjacent(...)`\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/OwnerEmail.java`** \u2014 made the class `public` and added `public static boolean isDisposableAdjacent(String email)`, reusing the existing `BLOCKED_DOMAINS` blocklist as the single source of truth. Since exact-blocklist domains are already rejected with a 400 at create time (so a stored owner never carries one), \"disposable-adjacent\" catches the near-misses that slip past that gate: a domain sharing a blocked provider's base label but not itself blocked (e.g. `mailinator.net`, `sub.tempmail.com`).\n\n**A note on interpretation:** the spec text in the test's Javadoc is truncated in the repo and no test exercises the `true` cases \u2014 only `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` (a clean owner \u2192 `false`). I mapped the two clearly-existing signals to their persisted flags, and defined \"disposable-adjacent\" against the existing blocklist as described above. If your intended definition of \"disposable-adjacent\" differs (e.g. a different similarity metric or a separate list), point me at it and I'll adjust `isDisposableAdjacent`.",
